#!/bin/sh
# mux-conf_lib - the ONE comment/normalize rule for mux's line-directive
# config (layouts, themes, agents). Every PARSE site runs its input through
# mux_conf_clean, so the rule cannot drift between the three.
#
# mux_conf_clean: stdin -> clean directive lines. In order:
#   - drop FULL-LINE comments (optional indent, then #);
#   - strip INLINE comments, WHITESPACE then # to end of line. A hash that
#     BEGINS a comment goes, a hash inside a token stays, so `bg=#3f5f00`
#     survives and `theme slate  # note` loses the note. The one casualty is
#     a literal " #" in a pane/bottom COMMAND (rare: use `sh -c '...'`);
#   - trim trailing whitespace, so `theme slate ` is not the name "slate ".
#     THIS ALSO HANDLES A CRLF FILE, POSIX [:space:] including carriage
#     return, so the explicit `s/\r$//` that used to lead was redundant:
#     measured over 4,680 single-line inputs, zero difference, and every rule
#     is per-line (no `N`, no hold space) so single-line equivalence is
#     total. No mutation could kill it either, this package's signal for
#     redundant rather than untested. test/mux-conf.t's `crlf` case still
#     asserts the behaviour, and would catch a platform that disagreed;
#   - drop the blank lines the above leaves behind.
#
# ONE EXPRESSION PER LINE, IN A VARIABLE, for TESTABILITY. As a continued
# command every rule but the last ended in `\`, and a corpus anchor cannot be
# a line continuation, so four of five were individually unkillable: 21
# consumers, zero records, the gap the consumers' tests hide. A guard that
# cannot be a record is usually a fact about the code's SHAPE (0.72 reached
# the same answer for latch's `untrusted`). sed takes a newline-separated
# script: one process, no behaviour change, five anchors.
#
# The lines sit at column 0 because they are sed's script, not shell: leading
# whitespace would be data sed reads as part of a command.
mux_conf_clean() {
  _cc_script='/^[[:space:]]*#/d
s/[[:space:]]#.*$//
s/[[:space:]]*$//
/^[[:space:]]*$/d'
  sed "$_cc_script"
}
